N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition. Bar-by-bar audit: bar 1 (C7) marcato G4 quarter, then G-Bb-G-C5-Bb-G-Eb eighths falling through the blues scale; bar 2 (C7) F-F#-G4, the dotted half on G closing the call; bars 3-4 (C7) whole-bar rests, the first answer window; bar 5 (F7) the call rebuilt on F7, marcato C5 then Eb5-C5-A4-C5-A4-F4; bar 6 (F7) Ab-A-C5 dotted half, the same chromatic approach as bar 2; bars 7-8 (C7) rests, the second answer window; bar 9 (G7) marcato D5 then D5-B4-G4-F4-D4; bar 10 (F7) Eb-F-Ab-F-C5-A-F; bar 11 (C7) Eb-E-G-C5-Bb-G-Eb; bar 12 (C7) C4 dotted half and a quarter rest. Every bar totals eight eighths; written range C4-Eb5; largest leap a perfect fifth (F4-C5, bar 10). This replaces the batch draft, which had a B4-C4 major-seventh drop in bar 5, a B natural against F7 and a G7 chord under the final tonic. Form AAB: bars 5-6 restate the call over IV, bars 9-12 answer it. Originality check: no stop-time hits and no beat-one riff from the reference, no Dixon words, and the call is not the opening of any well-known blues head checked ("C Jam Blues", "Bags' Groove", "Tenor Madness", "Sonnymoon for Two", "Night Train"); falling blues-scale figures like bar 1 are common property of the idiom.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether two-bar answer windows are long enough for Grade 7 soloists at quarter note = 72-96, whether Eb5 in bar 5 is comfortable for the class's voices or recorders, and whether the swing style's comp sits well under a blues have not been tested. The history framing needs a blues educator's review; a classroom trial, a second score reader and a student listening panel remain required before publication.
